// `algebra` — the library's first classes, and the instances the other
// `std` modules' carriers earn (docs/traits-design.md, issues/traits.md).
//
// Everything here is sugar over records and globals: a `class` is the record
// its fields describe, an `instance` is a namespace of ordinary declarations
// with the record assembled at the end. Nothing new reaches the kernel.
//
// The operation is an INDEX, not a field
// (docs/operation-indexed-classes-design.md): `Monoid<Bool>(and)` and
// `Monoid<Bool>(or)` are different types, so both register as equals and
// nothing here has to nominate one.
//
// A `designation` says what an operator SYMBOL means at a carrier
// (docs/operators-onto-classes-design.md), never which structure a carrier
// has. The symbol is a spelling of the index, so `sum_all` and `mconcat` are
// one fold reached two ways. Applied, a symbol reads its carrier off its
// operand; passed — `mconcat(++, xss)` — off the carrier the position expects
// (docs/bare-symbol-checking-carrier-design.md). One sentence, two directions.

import bool;
import logic;
import nat;
import list;
import option;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The classes.

// A monoid: an associative operation with a two-sided unit. `op` is the
// class's index, so `A` and `op` together identify the structure, and the
// unit — determined by the operation, definitionally at every concrete
// instance — stays a field (design §2.1).
// The heads write `op as ∙` (issues/binder-symbol-as-notation.md): the
// declared parameter is still `op` — the empty constraint slot binds that
// name, a linked slot refers by the caller's own, and the registry and the
// diagnostics never see a symbol — and `∙` is the spelling of that same index
// inside the class body. `∙` is the RESERVED GENERIC operator: nothing
// designates it at any carrier and it has no fallback name, so away from a
// binder that named it it cannot mean anything, which is what makes it the
// right symbol for bare semigroup/monoid theory where `+` would whisper
// commutativity over `append`.
class Semigroup<A>(op as ∙: A → A → A) {
    assoc: (x, y, z: A) → (x ∙ y) ∙ z = x ∙ (y ∙ z),
}

class Monoid<A>(op as ∙: A → A → A) extends Semigroup<A>(op) {
    unit: A,
    unit_l: (x: A) → unit ∙ x = x,
    unit_r: (x: A) → x ∙ unit = x,
}

// The commutative monoid: one chain, at every operation.
class CommMonoid<A>(op as ∙: A → A → A) extends Monoid<A>(op) {
    comm: (x, y: A) → x ∙ y = y ∙ x,
}

// The group: a monoid whose every element has an inverse. Added by the `Int`
// round (issues/std-int-encoding.md, which asked "worth checking whether `Group`
// should exist before this lands" — this is the answer, and it is yes), and
// `Int` under `add` is its first and so far ONLY carrier: `Nat` and `Bool` have
// no inverses under either of their operations, and `append` has none at all.
//
// The laws are stated over the INHERITED `unit`, which is what makes this a
// chain member rather than a new class with a copied unit field. Note what an
// instance therefore does NOT write: `instance Group<Int>(add)` supplies `inv`
// and its two laws only, and the monoid half comes from the canonical
// `CommMonoid<Int>(add)`. Writing the inherited fields again is refused unless
// the two terms are CONVERTIBLE, and two proofs of one equation are generally
// not — proof relevance means an override owes something a prover cannot supply
// (issues/inherited-field-override-owes-conversion.md).
class Group<A>(op as ∙: A → A → A) extends Monoid<A>(op) {
    inv: A → A,
    inv_l: (x: A) → inv(x) ∙ x = unit,
    inv_r: (x: A) → x ∙ inv(x) = unit,
}

// A value every type of the class has. No laws: `Default` is the class that
// exists to be *found*, which is the whole content of `or_default`.
class Default<A> {
    default: A,
}

// The canonical interpretation of a natural numeral at a carrier. Numeral
// syntax keeps its `Nat` default, but in a checking position at `A` the
// elaborator resolves this dictionary and applies `ofNat` to the written
// natural. One embedding per carrier; laws about it belong to that carrier's
// own API rather than to notation.
class Numeral<A> {
    ofNat: Nat → A,
}

// A decided order — and as of 2026-08-16 a **propositional** one
// (docs/nat-order-design.md §3). `le` returns a `Type`, the law is the
// relation itself rather than an equation with `True`, and the decision moved
// into its own field.
//
// The version this replaces was `le: A → A → Bool` with
// `le_refl: (x: A) → le(x, x) = True`, matching the former Boolean-reflection
// spelling `std/nat` then used. Both halves of that are retired by
// issues/class-ops-prop-vs-bool.md: no `Bool` in a specification, no `= True`
// anywhere. What is NOT retired is deciding — `max_of` still has to pick a
// value — so the decision it used to get from the `Bool` is now a third field
// returning `P ∨ ¬P`, which is the interface issues/decide-class.md will
// generalize. That class landed 2026-08-17 and this field stayed, which is the
// outcome the sentence above predicted: a `le_dec` field is a local decision
// about `Cmp`'s own `le`, an abstract relation with no instance to resolve
// through, so nothing here has a goal for `Decide` to answer. What changed for
// `Decide` is at the concrete relations — `std/nat`'s `Le` now has an instance
// generic in both its values (docs/value-generic-instances-design.md) — and
// none of the consumers below moved, as promised.
//
// The field is `le` and NOT `leb`, the name `std/nat` gives its decision
// procedure. A class field sharing a name with a global in scope can never be
// reached by a receiver: `x.leb(y)` is ambiguous between the member and the
// bare call, and the elaborator says so by name rather than picking. Naming a
// field is therefore a *library* decision about the ambient namespace, not a
// local one (issues/traits.md).
//
// `Cmp` and `Default` take no index: their structure is identified by the
// carrier alone, and indexing is opt-in per class (design §2.1).
class Cmp<A> {
    le: A → A → Type,
    le_refl: (x: A) → le(x, x),
    le_dec: (x, y: A) → Or<le(x, y), Not(le(x, y))>,
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Instances at the library's carriers.
//
// Every one of these is written in LITERAL form — `field := global` — because
// every fact it needs was already proved in the module that owns the carrier
// (docs/traits-design.md §3). Where a field is a one-liner nobody would name
// (`unit_r` for `add`, which is `refl`), it is written inline.
//
// Both `Bool` monoids register, as equals. Neither is "the" one, because no
// goal with an unpinned operation ever reaches the registry (design §5).

instance CommMonoid<Nat>(Nat::add) {
    unit := 0,
    assoc := Nat::add_assoc,
    unit_l := Nat::add_left_zero,
    unit_r := λ (x: Nat) => refl,
    comm := Nat::add_comm,
}

// The second `Nat` monoid, and the case that shows the unit field earns its
// keep: `1` is not a constructor of anything the class knows about, it is just
// a value, so nothing about `unit: A` had to bend to hold it. Worth reading
// beside `add`'s instance for the reversal — there `unit_r` is the free side
// and `unit_l` the lemma; here it is the other way round, and `unit_r` is
// filled by an `add` lemma, because `mul(x, 1)` reduces to `add(0, x)`.
instance CommMonoid<Nat>(Nat::mul) {
    unit := 1,
    assoc := Nat::mul_assoc,
    unit_l := Nat::mul_left_one,
    unit_r := Nat::add_left_zero,
    comm := Nat::mul_comm,
}

instance CommMonoid<Bool>(Bool::and) {
    unit := True,
    assoc := Bool::and_assoc,
    unit_l := Bool::and_true_l,
    unit_r := Bool::and_true_r,
    comm := Bool::and_comm,
}

instance CommMonoid<Bool>(Bool::or) {
    unit := False,
    assoc := Bool::or_assoc,
    unit_l := Bool::or_false_l,
    unit_r := Bool::or_false_r,
    comm := Bool::or_comm,
}

// The generic instance: one declaration, an instance at every element type.
// The chain is one class per law, and `append` stops at `Monoid`.
instance<A> Monoid<List<A>>(List::append) {
    unit := Nil,
    assoc := List::append_assoc,
    unit_l := λ (xs: List<A>) => refl,
    unit_r := List::append_nil,
}

instance Default<Nat> { default := 0 }
instance Default<Bool> { default := False }
instance<A> Default<List<A>> { default := Nil }
instance<A> Default<Option<A>> { default := None }

instance Numeral<Nat> { ofNat := λ (n: Nat) => n }

instance Cmp<Nat> { le := Nat::Le, le_refl := Nat::le_refl, le_dec := Nat::le_dec }
instance Cmp<Bool> { le := Bool::Le, le_refl := Bool::le_refl, le_dec := Bool::le_dec }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The consumers: written once against a class, used at every carrier.

// The fold every monoid is for, in the LINKED form (design §4.1): the
// operation is a written parameter and the constraint's index slot says it is
// the same one, so callers choose the structure positionally and nothing is
// searched for. `A.unit` is answered by the binder in scope.
//
// The parameter carries `as ∙` too, for the same reason the class heads do and
// with the same consequence: nothing outside changes — the constraint is still
// the plain linked slot naming `op`, callers still pass positionally — and the
// fold's own step reads as the operation it is.
fn mconcat<A: Monoid(op)>(self xs: List<A>, op as ∙: A → A → A) → A {
    match xs {
        Nil => A.unit,
        Cons(h, t) => h ∙ t.mconcat(op),
    }
}

// `list_fold` exposes the same consumer as a first-class algebra. This is the
// first free-monoid connection in the concrete-algebra experiment: the monoid
// dictionary supplies precisely the Nil and Cons clauses, and the established
// `mconcat` API is recovered pointwise rather than replaced.
fn monoid_list_alg<A: Monoid(op)>(op: A → A → A) → ListAlg<A, A> {
    ListAlg<A, A> { nil: A.unit, cons: op }
}

theorem list_fold_monoid<A: Monoid(op)>(op: A → A → A, xs: List<A>):
    ListAlg::list_fold(monoid_list_alg(op), xs) = xs.mconcat(op)
{
    ListAlg::list_fold_is_foldr(op, A.unit, xs)
}

// Proved once against the class; its uses below are at unrelated carriers and
// neither re-proves anything. `A.unit_r` is a **proof** field projected from
// the type — a type projection is an ordinary field projection, so an
// operation and a law are the same move.
theorem mconcat_single<A: Monoid(op)>(self x: A, op: A → A → A):
    ([x]).mconcat(op) = x
{
    A.unit_r(x)
}

// The order consumer: the class's `le_dec` decides which of two values to
// keep. The branch is hoisted into a separate `fn` because a `match` on a
// stuck scrutinee is unreachable from the goal, and that `fn` is
// `logic::or_pick`, because what is being branched on is a disjunction rather
// than a `Bool`. `list_algos`'s `insert` used to hoist its own `ins_pick` for
// the same reason and now calls `or_pick` directly (2026-08-17) — one hoisted
// branch for the whole library, which is what making it generic was for.
//
// This replaced `fn cmp_pick<A>(b: Bool, x, y: A) → A` when `Cmp` went
// propositional. The proof side got SIMPLER in the swap and it is worth
// saying why: `cmp_pick`'s lemma needed a `j` to rewrite the stuck `Bool` to
// `True` before the branch would reduce, whereas `or_pick`'s motive reaches
// its own scrutinee directly and the refuting arm is closed by the caller's
// proof. No equation, no transport.

// Written in the INLINE constraint form (issues/requires-placement-syntax.md
// §R2-A): none of these names the dictionary — `x.le(y)` dispatches from the
// receiver and `A.le_refl(x)` projects from the type — so `<A: Cmp>` says the
// whole of what is true about `A`.
//
// `A.le_refl` is on a **type** subject, which is what keeps it unambiguous
// against `std/nat`'s ambient `le_refl` global: the receiver reading would put
// a type in `le_refl`'s `Nat` slot, and the declaration refuses that
// (docs/traits-type-projection-design.md §4).
fn max_of<A: Cmp>(x, y: A) → A { or_pick(A.le_dec(x, y), x, y) }

// `max_of_true`'s successor, and the rename is the content: the hypothesis is
// no longer "the comparison returned `True`" but the RELATION ITSELF. That is
// the whole of what going propositional buys a caller — `p : x.le(y)` is the
// fact you have, where `p : x.le(y) = True` was a fact about a decision
// procedure you had to run first.
theorem max_of_le<A: Cmp>(x, y: A, p: A.le(x, y)): max_of(x, y) = y {
    or_pick_dec(A.le_dec(x, y), x, y, p)
}

theorem max_of_self<A: Cmp>(x: A): max_of(x, x) = x {
    max_of_le(x, x, A.le_refl(x))
}

// `Group`'s generic consequence, and the reason the class is worth declaring
// rather than leaving `Int`'s three lemmas loose: an inverse is UNIQUE, proved
// once here and reachable at every group. `std/int`'s `neg_unique` is this
// theorem with the dictionary found by resolution and nothing re-proved.
theorem op_inv_unique<A: Group(∙)>(x, y: A, h: x ∙ y = A.unit): y = A.inv(x) {
    apply trans;
    give sym(A.unit_l(y));
    apply trans;
    give j(sym(A.inv_l(x)), λ w q => A.unit ∙ y = w ∙ y, refl);
    apply trans;
    give A.assoc(A.inv(x), x, y);
    apply trans;
    give j(h, λ w q => A.inv(x) ∙ (x ∙ y) = A.inv(x) ∙ w, refl);
    A.unit_r(A.inv(x))
}

// `Default` earns its keep here: the caller of `or_default` names no
// dictionary and no fallback value. In the inline constraint form, like
// `max_of` above — nothing names the dictionary, since `A.default` projects
// the value straight from the type.
fn or_default<A: Default>(self o: Option<A>) → A { o.unwrap_or(A.default) }

// The chain's generic consequence: a right unit follows from commutativity and
// the left one, so `Monoid`'s `unit_r` is redundant *for a commutative
// monoid*. Written in the SYMBOL form
// (docs/operators-onto-classes-design.md §5): the `+` here is the bound index,
// not an ambient operator, and every corollary below pins it **by unification**
// from its own goal — one proof, three structures, two of them on one carrier.
// A designation supplies an index nothing else pinned; it never overrides one,
// which is why the binder's `+` is never asked what it means at `Bool`.
theorem op_unit_of_comm<A: CommMonoid(+)>(x: A): x + A.unit = x {
    trans(A.comm(x, A.unit), A.unit_l(x))
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The specializations, one per way an instance can be found.

// The fold under whatever `+` means here
// (docs/operators-onto-classes-design.md §5). Inside this declaration `+` IS
// the bound index, so the two folds are one term; at a call site the index is
// filled from the designation at the caller's carrier.
fn sum_all<A: Monoid(+)>(self xs: List<A>) → A { xs.mconcat(+) }

// One declaration, two carriers, and no caller names an operation. The
// second is at an **open** element type, so its goal is
// `Monoid<List<A>>(append)` with `A` the caller's own parameter, answered by
// backchaining through `instance<A> Monoid<List<A>>(append)`.
fn sum(self ns: List<Nat>) → Nat { ns.sum_all() }

// The monoid here is at `List<A>`, which is no tyvar, so no constraint slot
// could ever name a symbol for it — and yet `++` is passed bare, because the
// slot's own type says the carrier is `List<A>` and the generic designation
// answers there (docs/bare-symbol-checking-carrier-design.md). Same term as
// `mconcat(append, xss)`, which is what `concat_single` below still proves.
fn concat<A>(self xss: List<List<A>>) → List<A> { xss.mconcat(++) }

// The `Bool` folds are still NOT `sum_all` — `+` means nothing at `Bool` — but
// each now writes its own symbol, and the bare symbol takes its meaning from
// the carrier the slot expects (`Bool`), not from any structure being preferred
// there. There is still no third fold beside them: a second declaration of the
// same fold under a second symbol is duplication. Side by side they are the
// sentence that nothing was nominated — two symbols, two structures, one
// carrier, both registered, both reachable, neither the default. The positional
// spelling stays legal forever, and the `_single` lemmas below still use it.
fn any_of(self bs: List<Bool>) → Bool { bs.mconcat(||) }

fn all_of(self bs: List<Bool>) → Bool { bs.mconcat(&&) }

// The class lemma at three carriers. None re-proves anything: each is
// `mconcat_single` with the dictionary found through the registry, and the two
// `Bool` folds are different functions. `concat_single` is the Wf-carrier
// regression for issues/class-lemma-instantiation-fuel.md: comparing the
// instantiated generic statement used to re-walk shared type closures
// until conversion exhausted its full budget.
theorem sum_single(self n: Nat): ([n]).sum() = n { n.mconcat_single(Nat::add) }

theorem all_of_single(self b: Bool): ([b]).all_of() = b { b.mconcat_single(Bool::and) }

theorem any_of_single(self b: Bool): ([b]).any_of() = b { b.mconcat_single(Bool::or) }

theorem concat_single<A>(self xs: List<A>): ([xs]).concat() = xs {
    xs.mconcat_single(List::append)
}

// The generic law, spent three times. Each goal names its operation — that is
// what a law is — so unification pins the index and the ground goal hits the
// registry key. Two of the three are the same carrier.
//
// All three are written in the notation: no binder names a symbol here, so the
// designation at the operand's carrier answers (resolution rung 2) and the
// goals ARE `add(x, 0) = x`, `and(x, True) = x` and `or(x, False) = x`.
// Resolution happens at the statement; the index is then pinned from the
// resolved goal by unification.
theorem nat_add_zero(x: Nat): x + 0 = x { op_unit_of_comm(x) }

theorem bool_and_true(x: Bool): x && True = x { op_unit_of_comm(x) }

theorem bool_or_false(x: Bool): x || False = x { op_unit_of_comm(x) }
